# Mobile Data Loss Prevention (DLP) Assessment

Context
- Seneca Polytechnic
- Course: CYT230 Mobile Application Security Assessment
- Project 2

Lab work
- Android emulator connected via ADB.
- Test DPC installed and provisioned as Device Owner.
- "Disable screen capture" policy enabled, then verified by attempting a screenshot; the system blocked the capture.

Static analysis
- MobSF scan of an APK [TODO: confirm APK name or source].
- Findings:
  - dangerous permissions: coarse location, contacts, accounts
  - manifest issues: outdated Android versions supported, debugging enabled, backup allowed
  - StrandHogg 2.0 task-hijacking flag on the PostLogin activity
- No hardcoded credentials, insecure API calls, risky crypto, exported activities, or network config issues were flagged.
- A clean scan does not prove the app is secure.

Research summary
- Layered DLP model across hardware/TEE, OS/MDM, application/MAM, network, radio, and physical layers.
- Case studies on T-Mobile 2021, Pegasus, SHAREit, and Strava.
- Six recommendations.

Skills demonstrated
- MDM policy enforcement
- Android Device Owner provisioning
- ADB
- MobSF static analysis
- OWASP Mobile Top 10 mapping
- DLP policy design

